  m_Name: KalpanTurbineData
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  engineName: Kalpan Turbine
  engineDescription: A Kaplan Turbine is a reaction-type hydraulic turbine that converts
    the energy of flowing water into mechanical energy and is particularly suitable
    for low-head, high-flow applications. It operates on the principle of pressure
    and velocity energy conversion as water passes axially through adjustable runner
    blades. Its major components include the spiral casing, guide vanes, runner,
    adjustable blades, shaft, hub, and draft tube. During operation, water enters
    the turbine through the casing and is directed toward the runner by the guide
    vanes. The water transfers its energy to the rotating runner, while the adjustable
    blade pitch allows the turbine to maintain good efficiency under varying flow
    conditions. The draft tube then slows the water and helps recover part of its
    kinetic energy. Kaplan turbines are widely used in hydroelectric power plants.
    From an engineering perspective, hydraulic efficiency, blade pitch control, cavitation,
    head losses, flow rate, and draft-tube performance are important for reliable
    operation.
  engineCategory: Engineering
